        "heading": "2 RELATED WORKS & BACKGROUND",
        "text": "Sequential LVMs: Related works on learning a latent dynamic function from high-dimensional time-series have been presented under different terminologies, such as variational latent recurrent\nmodels (Chung et al., 2015; Rubanova et al., 2019; Yildiz et al., 2020), deep state space models (SSM) (Krishnan et al., 2017; Karl et al., 2017; Fraccaro et al., 2017; Rangapuram et al., 2018; Li et al., 2019; Klushyn et al., 2021), and deterministic encoding-decoding models (Botev et al., 2021). Among the first is the variational recurrent neural networks (VRNN) (Chung et al., 2015), followed by a series of deep SSM models (Krishnan et al., 2017; Li et al., 2019) focused on modeling the dependence of the posterior and transitional density of the latent state zk on past latent states z<k and observations x<k (Fig. 1A). Alternatively, there has been a line of deep SSMs that aims to infer the parameters of the latent dynamic function instead (Karl et al., 2017; Fraccaro et al., 2017; Rangapuram et al., 2018; Klushyn et al., 2021). Till now all existing approaches assumed linear latent dynamics, where the linear transition matrix at each time frame k is modeled as a linear combination of a set of global matrices. The linear coefficients are time-varying and to be inferred from observations x\u2264k as illustrated in Fig. 1B1. In both formulations, the reliance on inferred time-varying inputs to the latent dynamic function (states or parameters) reduces its ability to forecast without near-term observations.\nIn parallel, a set of models (Fig. 1B2) have been presented that aims to learn a latent dynamic function that forecast a sequence using only an inferred initial state, in stochastic (Rubanova et al., 2019; Yildiz et al., 2020) or deterministic forms (Botev et al., 2021). The resulting latent dynamic function is strong at forecasting, albeit only a single function is learned at a time. We build on and advance this formulation to learning to learn a dynamic-specific function from few-shot observations.\nModeling switching dynamics in sequential LVMs, often based on the formulation in Fig. 1A, shares the presented idea of using context variables to control the latent dynamics (Becker-Ehmck et al., 2019; Linderman et al., 2017). They however are concerned with the switching of dynamics within a time-series, whereas we are interested in learning to learn dynamics from k-shot support series.\nSequential neural processes (SNPs) are also sequential LVMs based on the formulation in Fig. 1A (Singh et al., 2019; Qin et al., 2019). Similar to the presented work, SNPs are underlined by Bayesian meta-learning. They however are concerned with using partially observed frames within a time-series as support sets, for the query of unobserved frames in the same time-series.\nAutoregressive dynamic modeling: Autoregressive models are also popular for modeling and forecasting dynamics, especially for approximating physics-based simulations (Wang et al., 2020; Pfaff et al., 2020). Some recent works have focused on the issue of generalizing across dynamics by, for instance, disentangling spatial and temporal modeling (Dona\u0300 et al., 2020) or learning dynamic-specific functions in addition to a global dynamic function (Yin et al., 2021). None of these works considered meta-learning or few-shot adaption of dynamic functions as a solution, nor can autoregressive models support controlled generation of synthesized time-series as we will demonstrate in Section 5.\nGeneral few-shot learning: Few-shot learning has seen substantial progress with static data. Recent types of meta-models include weight initialization (Finn et al., 2017; Yoon et al., 2018), model optimizers (Ravi & Larochelle, 2016), and feed-forward models to condition (Garnelo et al., 2018) or parameterize the primary networks (Bertinetto et al., 2016; Sung et al., 2018). Compared to the former two, feed-forward meta-models replace test-time optimization with simple feed-forward passes using support data. It thus constitutes the basis of the presented few-shot forecasting methods.\nFew-shot time-series forecasting: Meta-learning is well studied in univariate time-series forecasting (Montero-Manso et al., 2020) including recent deep-learning advances (Oreshkin et al., 2021). Fewshot forecasting for high-dimensional time-series, however, has not been attempted to our knowledge."
