      {
        "heading": "6 FORECASTING OF CARDIAC ELECTRICAL DYNAMICS",
        "text": "Finally, we considered the modeling and forecasting of more complex dynamics describing realworld physics of the propagation of electrical waves in the heart. This physics can be described by reaction-diffusion partial differential equations (PDEs) as below (Aliev & Panfilov, 1996):\n\u2202u \u2202t = \u2207(D\u2207u)\u2212 cu(u\u2212 \u03b8)(u\u2212 1)\u2212 uv; \u2202v \u2202t = \u03b5(u, v)(\u2212v \u2212 cu(u\u2212 \u03b8 \u2212 1)). (9)\nWhen solved over the 3D geometrical mesh of the heart, u over space and time describes the time series of high-dimensional 3D electrical potential on the heart. Parameters \u03b5, c, \u03b8 and D reflect the material properties of an individual\u2019s heart and influence the obsesved dynamics. Emerging neural approximations of this PDE-based physics simulation (Fresca et al., 2021) is typically tied to a specific configuration of these PDE parameters. Here, we test the ability of the presented framework to generate and forecast a diverse set of this physics-based dynamics using few-shot support series.\nData: We solved Equation (9) in a heart mesh simulating 3D electrical propagation originating from various locations in the heart, with a total of 15 settings of material properties representing 15 dynamic systems with different locations of injury to the heart muscle. We use 9 dynamics in meta-training, 3 in meta-validation, and 3 in meta-testing, with disjoint time-series with different initial conditions (meta-training: 450; meta-test: 2,020). We let k vary between 1 and 5.\nModels: We adopted a base encoding-decoding architecture with a graph-CNN encoder/decoder and an ODE-GRU latent dynamic function zk = f(z<k;\u03b8z) similar to that described in (Jiang et al., 2021). We trained it with a global \u03b8z (global GRU), an individual \u03b8z for each material parameter setting (single-dynamics GRU), a conditioned f(zi\u22121, c;\u03b8z) with c encoded from individual training series (instance-specific GRU), and the presented framework (meta GRU). We further added a strong baseline using the original PDEs (9) with material parameters optimized by a SOTA approach from k-shot support series as described in (Dhamala et al., 2018) (personalized virtual heart / PVH).\nMetrics: We used quantitative metrics of MSE and spatial correlation coefficient (CC) between the forecast and actual time-series of 3D electrical propagation.\nResults: Fig. 6A provides an example illustrating how electrical wave propagates around locations of muscle injury specific to the setting of material properties (column 1). Meta-GRU showed the most accurate details of this propagation block compared to its various baseline counterparts. This gain in forecasting performance is quantitatively summarized in Fig. 6B across all meta-test time-series, and an additional visual example can be found in Appendix E. Note that the PVH represents a strong baseline relying on solving the actual PDEs in Equations (9), which took on average 5 minutes each versus 0.24 seconds by the meta-GRU; moreover, to optimize PDE parameters on average required 100 calls to the PDEs, versus 0.032 seconds for meta-GRU to adapt to specific dynamics."
